671>>>>>>>>>
671>>>>>>>>>// This returns the appropriate window handle to be used when you need a handle for a modal windows
671>>>>>>>>>// dialog (e.g. Message box, Open file dialog). The handle returned insures that the proper handle
671>>>>>>>>>// is used that will provide the required modality. The runtime uses the same logic when creating
671>>>>>>>>>// DataFlex Modal objects
671>>>>>>>>>
671>>>>>>>>>Function gOwnerWindowHandle Global Handle hoObj Returns Handle
673>>>>>>>>>    Handle hWnd hwPrnt hwTest hwOwner
673>>>>>>>>>    Boolean bEnabled
673>>>>>>>>>    
673>>>>>>>>>    If (hoObj<=Desktop) Begin  // if no focus, use main_window
675>>>>>>>>>        Get Main_Window of Desktop to hoObj
676>>>>>>>>>    End
676>>>>>>>>>>
676>>>>>>>>>    // find the first container handle.
676>>>>>>>>>    If (hoObj>desktop) Begin
678>>>>>>>>>        Get Container_Handle of hoObj to hWnd
679>>>>>>>>>        While (hWnd=0 and hoObj>Desktop)
683>>>>>>>>>            Get Parent of hoObj to hoObj
684>>>>>>>>>            Get Container_Handle of hoObj to hWnd
685>>>>>>>>>        Loop
686>>>>>>>>>>
686>>>>>>>>>    End
686>>>>>>>>>>
686>>>>>>>>>    
686>>>>>>>>>    // 12.0 change: check to make sure we have the correct owner handle for message box. If the message
686>>>>>>>>>    // box is getting used within a toolpanel (or any top level panel that is not the main_window panel and is
686>>>>>>>>>    // not a modal panel) then we want to use the main_window handle. When the main panel is used it will disable all other
686>>>>>>>>>    // top level panels (this is 12.0 r/t change). In addition we only want to do this if windows are not already disabled.
686>>>>>>>>>    // Therefore, start with the current target handle and search its parents. If the handle or any parent is disabled, we
686>>>>>>>>>    // don't have to do anything. Check parents until you hit a top level window (parent=0). If this top-level parent has a
686>>>>>>>>>    // gw_owner it is not the main-window (e.g. it's a toolpanel). These we must adjust. We want to use the main-window as the
686>>>>>>>>>    // handle, which will give us the proper modality.
686>>>>>>>>>    // A note about GetParent. This windows message returns the parent or the owner (which is different than
686>>>>>>>>>    // the gw_owner). Top-level modeless windows (panel, toolpanel), return a parent of 0 (and not the gw_owner). Modal panels return
686>>>>>>>>>    // the panel that owns them, which is presumably already disabled. Therefore using GetParent works for our needs.
686>>>>>>>>>    If hWnd Begin
688>>>>>>>>>        Move hWnd to hwPrnt
689>>>>>>>>>        Repeat
689>>>>>>>>>>
689>>>>>>>>>            Move (IsWindowEnabled(hwPrnt)) to bEnabled
690>>>>>>>>>            If bEnabled Begin // if we encounter a disabled parent, we are done ... we don't change anything
692>>>>>>>>>                Move (GetParent(hwPrnt)) to hwTest
693>>>>>>>>>                If (hwTest=0) Begin // no parent, a top level window
695>>>>>>>>>                    Move (GetWindow(hwPrnt,GW_Owner)) to hwOwner // if this has a gw_owner, we will use that owner
696>>>>>>>>>                    // If top level window, either return it's owner or if no owner, that window
696>>>>>>>>>                    // If no owner it is probably a panel (possibly the main window), if a owner it is probably a toolpanel
696>>>>>>>>>                    Move (If(hwOwner,hwOwner,hwPrnt)) to hWnd // toolpanels have a gw_owner, Panels do not.
697>>>>>>>>>                    // so this adjusts toolpanels which is what we want
697>>>>>>>>>                    //If hwOwner Begin
697>>>>>>>>>                    //Move hwOwner to hWnd
697>>>>>>>>>                    //End
697>>>>>>>>>                End
697>>>>>>>>>>
697>>>>>>>>>                Move hwTest to hwPrnt
698>>>>>>>>>            End
698>>>>>>>>>>
698>>>>>>>>>        Until (not(bEnabled) or hwPrnt=0)
700>>>>>>>>>    End
700>>>>>>>>>>
700>>>>>>>>>    Function_Return hWnd
701>>>>>>>>>End_Function
